In general, yes, but gnomish healer is one race/role combination where
this might not be the case.  Surviving to minetown might actually be
easier than surviving long enough to get both MR and reflection
thereafter.  I haven't looked at the data, however.

> In return, however, it demands that you get all the way to Minetown as
> an XL1 character, which is much harder than getting there in one piece
> after gaining a few levels
Don't underestimate the benefit of spawning a whole load of dungeon
levels while still at XL1, though.  A 72-HP cat can be a powerful
weapon into the late teen dungeon levels so long as the monster
difficulty is kept down by your own XL.  And that's if you aren't
lucky enough to find a magic whistle and a polytrap on the way.

> and then once you do get there you're still
> rather behind in terms of gaining levels.
Once you're at that depth it takes very few kills (as low as 1-2 kills
per XL in some cases) to get you to level 5-7 once you start killing
things.

> The levels you've not gained
> would also have survivability benefits (ongoing ones, as well as on
> the way there), not least a rather substantial chunk of max HP.
This is the major killer in the protection racket (at least with
gnomes and dwarfs, where the mines are populated with plenty of
peaceful monsters) in my experience.  So long as healers are smart
enough to use their starting potions to up their max HP, this is
partly negated.  Having poison resistance also helps enormously, and
again healers (and a handful of others) have that advantage.  I guess
healers also start with plenty of cash, but as has been pointed out,
gold isn't usually in short supply.

I think the truth is a little more subtle than "dead end; has always
been."  Although players can eventually get good enough not to need to
rely on it, I suspect even Marvin might have found himself standing
next to the minetown priest at XL1 with at least 400 gold in some
games.


